/* Original objective-depth workbook, technical sources verified separately; not independent SME review. */
window.MD102_OBJECTIVE_DEPTH_R5=[{"id":"INF-001","terms":[["Registered","Workplace device identity normally associated with personal sign-in."],["Joined","Organizational device relationship where supported users sign in with Entra credentials."],["Hybrid joined","AD DS domain membership plus an Entra device identity."]],"mechanism":"Choose identity from ownership, Windows sign-in requirements and legacy resource needs. A directory relationship creates a device trust context, not a management channel. A successful sign-in or registration alone cannot establish Intune enrollment.","prereq":"Confirm supported OS, tenant and whether an AD DS relationship is actually required. Check identity, enrollment, entitlement and access separately.","scenario":"A personal PC must retain its local Windows sign-in. The organization only needs a work identity association.","choices":["Register the device; evaluate app protection and access separately.","Force hybrid join","Treat any directory object as Intune enrollment"],"why":["Choose identity from ownership, Windows sign-in requirements and legacy resource needs. A directory relationship creates a device trust context, not a management channel. A successful sign-in or registration alone cannot establish Intune enrollment.","Hybrid join adds a domain dependency that the scenario does not need.","Entra and Intune store separate state; verify both when required."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/devices/overview"},{"id":"INF-002","terms":[["dsregcmd","Windows command used to inspect device registration and join information."],["AzureAdJoined","Diagnostic flag for a Microsoft Entra joined relationship; not an Intune compliance flag."]],"mechanism":"Join establishes a cloud directory relationship. Supported sign-in flows can subsequently trigger automatic enrollment if eligibility, MDM scope and entitlement permit it. The Windows device state, Entra object and Intune managed record should correlate but are not interchangeable.","prereq":"Use an approved disposable Windows endpoint, eligible account and expected tenant. Inspect join and management state before and after; never join a personal production PC as a lab.","scenario":"A lab PC reports AzureAdJoined YES but does not appear in Intune. Which check is appropriate?","choices":["Inspect the tenant, MDM scope, enrollment eligibility and enrollment evidence separately.","Repeat join until Intune appears","Declare the PC compliant"],"why":["Join establishes a cloud directory relationship. Supported sign-in flows can subsequently trigger automatic enrollment if eligibility, MDM scope and entitlement permit it. The Windows device state, Entra object and Intune managed record should correlate but are not interchangeable.","Repeated join can create stale or duplicate records and does not fix enrollment prerequisites.","A join indicator does not establish managed or compliant state."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/devices/device-join-plan"},{"id":"INF-003","terms":[["Work account","An organizational identity added to a device or app."],["Ownership","Personal versus corporate context affecting supported enrollment and policy behavior."]],"mechanism":"Registration associates a work identity with a device and provides device context for organizational access. Some supported workflows may separately offer enrollment; choosing registration does not automatically grant full device management.","prereq":"Confirm BYOD policy, privacy, supported registration workflow, account and device state. Check the registered identity and whether a management record actually exists.","scenario":"An employee has a personal device registered in Entra and wants to know if IT can reset the entire device.","choices":["Check the actual management relationship and platform before making any claim about device-wide control.","Assume registration enables wipe","Assume no organizational policy can ever apply"],"why":["Registration associates a work identity with a device and provides device context for organizational access. Some supported workflows may separately offer enrollment; choosing registration does not automatically grant full device management.","Registration alone does not grant MDM remote actions.","App-based and access policies can still apply without full MDM where supported."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/devices/concept-device-registration"},{"id":"INF-004","terms":[["Dynamic membership","Group membership calculated from supported attributes and a membership rule."],["Evaluation latency","Time between an attribute/rule change and resulting group membership."]],"mechanism":"A dynamic rule describes eligible identity objects. Group evaluation creates membership; policy assignment consumes membership; Intune filters may further refine supported assignments. These are separate processing layers.","prereq":"Use supported rule properties, required licensing and a pilot group. Verify membership using intended included and excluded devices before assigning policy.","scenario":"A device meets the written dynamic rule, but the group has not yet listed it. What is the appropriate diagnostic?","choices":["Check the actual device attributes, rule validity and membership processing state.","Use a scope tag to force membership","Assign the profile to all devices"],"why":["A dynamic rule describes eligible identity objects. Group evaluation creates membership; policy assignment consumes membership; Intune filters may further refine supported assignments. These are separate processing layers.","Tags affect supported administrative visibility, not Entra group calculation.","Broad assignment defeats pilot containment."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/users/groups-dynamic-membership"},{"id":"INF-005","terms":[["MDM authority","The service responsible for managing applicable MDM workloads."],["Enrollment restriction","A configured condition that controls whether supported devices can enroll."]],"mechanism":"An enrollment request is evaluated against supported method, identity/license, platform and restrictions. A rejected enrollment is not equivalent to an assigned policy failure; the managed relationship may not exist yet.","prereq":"Confirm MDM authority, supported method, platform, ownership, assigned restrictions and user entitlement. Test both allowed and deliberately excluded fictional or disposable devices.","scenario":"A corporate Android device fails enrollment because the platform is blocked by an applicable restriction. What is the safest fix?","choices":["Inspect the specific restriction assignment and approved pilot exception, then retest.","Disable all enrollment restrictions globally","Create a BitLocker policy"],"why":["An enrollment request is evaluated against supported method, identity/license, platform and restrictions. A rejected enrollment is not equivalent to an assigned policy failure; the managed relationship may not exist yet.","Global removal affects unrelated devices and bypasses intended governance.","Windows encryption policy is unrelated to Android enrollment admission."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/device-enrollment/guide"},{"id":"INF-006","terms":[["MDM user scope","Which eligible users are in scope for supported automatic Windows enrollment."],["Enrollment diagnostics","Evidence about the attempt to establish the Windows management relationship."]],"mechanism":"Automatic enrollment depends on eligible identity/Windows scenario, scope and licensing. A join operation can succeed while automatic enrollment fails because a different service evaluates management prerequisites.","prereq":"Use an eligible Windows test VM/account, correct tenant, supported enrollment setup and network access. Verify separate Entra and Intune records and fresh check-in.","scenario":"A licensed pilot joins Entra, but the MDM user scope excludes the user. What must be addressed first?","choices":["Include the approved user in the relevant automatic enrollment scope and retry supported enrollment.","Change the user display name","Change the device compliance deadline"],"why":["Automatic enrollment depends on eligible identity/Windows scenario, scope and licensing. A join operation can succeed while automatic enrollment fails because a different service evaluates management prerequisites.","Display name does not control enrollment scope.","Compliance policy processing depends on the applicable management relationship."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/deploy-setup-step-1"},{"id":"INF-007","terms":[["User enrollment","Apple enrollment approach designed for supported personally owned scenarios with work-data separation."],["Supervision","Apple management state enabling additional corporate controls."]],"mechanism":"Personal Apple enrollment choices determine the extent of management and privacy separation. App-only protection is a separate option where the required app and access scenario support it. macOS and iOS/iPadOS methods are not identical.","prereq":"Check supported Apple OS, enrollment type, identity integration, Apple prerequisites and privacy requirements. Validate managed work data and permitted controls.","scenario":"IT only needs to protect data in a supported work app on a personal iPhone; full device management is unnecessary. Which choice should be evaluated?","choices":["App protection with the appropriate supported access design, or documented personal enrollment if additional device controls are required.","Force corporate supervision","Wipe the device before deciding"],"why":["Personal Apple enrollment choices determine the extent of management and privacy separation. App-only protection is a separate option where the required app and access scenario support it. macOS and iOS/iPadOS methods are not identical.","Supervision exceeds the stated app-data requirement.","Wipe is destructive and not a prerequisite for app protection."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/device-enrollment/apple/guide-ios-ipados"},{"id":"INF-008","terms":[["Dedicated device","Organization-owned Android device intended for a dedicated task."],["Work profile","Managed work container separating applicable work apps and data from personal use."]],"mechanism":"Android Enterprise management mode follows ownership and purpose: personal work profile, corporate work profile, fully managed or dedicated. The chosen mode limits supported controls; tokens and setup flows are mode-specific.","prereq":"Connect the supported Android enterprise integration; validate ownership, QR/token, platform support and restrictions. Use a disposable test device only.","scenario":"A kiosk has no assigned employee and must run a single approved business app. Which mode is appropriate?","choices":["Corporate-owned dedicated device enrollment.","Personally owned work profile","Corporate work profile for mixed personal use"],"why":["Android Enterprise management mode follows ownership and purpose: personal work profile, corporate work profile, fully managed or dedicated. The chosen mode limits supported controls; tokens and setup flows are mode-specific.","A personal work profile assumes personal ownership/user use.","Mixed-use work profile is not the intended single-purpose shared device design."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/device-enrollment/android/setup-dedicated"},{"id":"INF-009","terms":[["ADE","Apple Automated Device Enrollment, linking eligible organizational hardware to management during setup."],["Enrollment profile","A supported setup configuration assigned to the enrolled Apple device through the integration."]],"mechanism":"Apple Business integration assigns eligible devices to an MDM service. The organization synchronizes applicable enrollment records and assigns setup profiles. The device obtains enrollment configuration during the supported setup flow; supervision and user affinity depend on the scenario.","prereq":"Validate authorized Apple Business integration, token renewal, APNs prerequisites, device assignment and supported setup flow. Never reset a real device just to test.","scenario":"The corporate iPhone appears in Apple Business but not in the expected Intune enrollment profile. What should be checked?","choices":["Verify MDM server assignment, token sync and profile assignment for that specific device.","Change a Windows Autopilot profile","Assume reseller purchase itself enrolled it"],"why":["Apple Business integration assigns eligible devices to an MDM service. The organization synchronizes applicable enrollment records and assigns setup profiles. The device obtains enrollment configuration during the supported setup flow; supervision and user affinity depend on the scenario.","Autopilot is for Windows provisioning.","Purchase eligibility alone does not establish the Intune record and configuration."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/device-enrollment/apple/setup-automated-ios"},{"id":"INF-010","terms":[["Zero-touch","Google-supported automated setup for eligible organizational Android devices."],["Knox Mobile Enrollment","Samsung-supported automated enrollment workflow for eligible Samsung hardware."]],"mechanism":"Both can direct supported devices toward enterprise setup during provisioning, but hardware eligibility, portals, profiles and registration mechanics differ. The Intune Android Enterprise profile determines the resulting management configuration.","prereq":"Confirm reseller/device eligibility, correct enrollment profile/configuration, approved token, integration and network. Test a disposable device's first setup.","scenario":"A Samsung pilot has an eligible registration but starts normal setup rather than enterprise enrollment. What is the initial check?","choices":["Verify the KME device/profile assignment and applicable setup prerequisites.","Use an Apple ADE token","Assume any Android serial automatically enrolls"],"why":["Both can direct supported devices toward enterprise setup during provisioning, but hardware eligibility, portals, profiles and registration mechanics differ. The Intune Android Enterprise profile determines the resulting management configuration.","Apple integration cannot configure Samsung Android setup.","Device registration and profile assignment are distinct steps."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/device-enrollment/android/guide"},{"id":"INF-011","terms":[["Role definition","A set of allowed administrative actions."],["Role assignment","Connects role permissions to a member group and applicable scope."]],"mechanism":"An administrator may have multiple grants. A narrowly scoped Intune role should provide required actions only. Windows 365 features have their own applicable permissions and entitlements; revisit their scenario-specific roles in Level 12.","prereq":"Create a fictional/scoped test administrator, identify exact tasks and applicable service roles, and verify permitted/denied actions.","scenario":"A helpdesk employee needs sync and reporting access for a pilot group, not broad tenant control. What design fits?","choices":["Use the least-privileged supported role with appropriate membership and scope.","Grant Global Administrator","Assign a configuration profile to the employee"],"why":["An administrator may have multiple grants. A narrowly scoped Intune role should provide required actions only. Windows 365 features have their own applicable permissions and entitlements; revisit their scenario-specific roles in Level 12.","Global permissions exceed the task.","Device policy assignment is not administrator authorization."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/role-based-access-control/overview"},{"id":"INF-012","terms":[["Scope group","Applicable users/devices an assigned Intune role may manage."],["Scope tag","Label used in supported role assignments to limit object visibility."]],"mechanism":"RBAC permissions define allowed verbs, scope groups define managed population, and scope tags affect supported resource visibility. Multiple grants can expand effective access, so verify positive and negative cases.","prereq":"Identify role, assigned admins, managed population and applicable tags. Test visible and hidden objects without granting broader privileges.","scenario":"An East-region helpdesk sees West-region devices unexpectedly. What is the first investigation?","choices":["Inspect overlapping role assignments, effective scope groups and applicable tags.","Remove West devices from Intune","Add a policy description saying East only"],"why":["RBAC permissions define allowed verbs, scope groups define managed population, and scope tags affect supported resource visibility. Multiple grants can expand effective access, so verify positive and negative cases.","Deleting records is destructive and not a permissions fix.","A description does not enforce administrative scope."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/role-based-access-control/overview"},{"id":"INF-013","terms":[["Multi Admin Approval","An approval workflow for supported Intune changes subject to configured access policies."],["Approver","A separate eligible administrator who reviews a requested change where required."]],"mechanism":"A configured approval policy can defer supported administrative changes until reviewed. It adds separation of duties rather than replacing RBAC, licensing or the need for change justification and audit.","prereq":"Confirm supported object type, requester permissions, separate authorized approver and scope. Use harmless lab change; capture request and disposition.","scenario":"A covered change is pending approval. The requester has another more privileged account. What should happen?","choices":["Use the prescribed independent approval workflow and record the audit evidence.","Bypass approval through the other account","Recreate the same policy with a misleading name"],"why":["A configured approval policy can defer supported administrative changes until reviewed. It adds separation of duties rather than replacing RBAC, licensing or the need for change justification and audit.","Bypass defeats separation of duties.","Renaming is not authorized review and can create duplicate objects."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/role-based-access-control/multi-admin-approval"},{"id":"INF-014","terms":[["Compliance rule","A requirement evaluated for a supported managed device."],["Noncompliance action","Configured response to a noncompliant condition, possibly after a grace period."]],"mechanism":"Compliance evaluates reported posture against applicable requirements. Configuration profiles may implement a setting; compliance assesses it. Aggregated compliance can be consumed by Conditional Access, but does not automatically enforce every configuration or block every app.","prereq":"Choose supported platform rules and scope; verify policy applicability, action timing, actual device state and report freshness.","scenario":"A Windows device fails an encryption compliance rule. What should IT investigate?","choices":["Check actual encryption, applicable configuration policy and the compliance reason, then retest.","Assume compliance policy automatically encrypted it","Disable every compliance requirement"],"why":["Compliance evaluates reported posture against applicable requirements. Configuration profiles may implement a setting; compliance assesses it. Aggregated compliance can be consumed by Conditional Access, but does not automatically enforce every configuration or block every app.","Compliance detects rather than generally configuring encryption.","Removing requirements hides the failure instead of correcting the cause."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/device-security/compliance/overview"},{"id":"INF-015","terms":[["Grant control","A requirement evaluated by Conditional Access for applicable sign-ins."],["Report-only","Policy evaluation without enforcing that configured access policy."]],"mechanism":"The service evaluates user/resource conditions, device context and grant requirements at supported sign-ins. Device compliance is an input, not a substitute for identity or the Conditional Access policy. Report-only outcomes predict applicable enforcement but do not prove it is safe to enable broadly.","prereq":"Verify eligible licensing, pilot identities/resource, current compliance, emergency access exclusions and sign-in logs. Never target all users in a lab.","scenario":"A noncompliant device can still open a protected cloud app while the only matching policy is report-only. Why?","choices":["Report-only evaluates and records outcomes without enforcing the grant.","Compliance is always enforced immediately by Intune alone","Assign a scope tag to block sign-in"],"why":["The service evaluates user/resource conditions, device context and grant requirements at supported sign-ins. Device compliance is an input, not a substitute for identity or the Conditional Access policy. Report-only outcomes predict applicable enforcement but do not prove it is safe to enable broadly.","Intune compliance alone does not impose every resource's access requirements.","Scope tags do not decide access to cloud apps."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/device-security/conditional-access-integration/device-based-policies"},{"id":"INF-016","terms":[["Windows Hello for Business","Key-based organizational Windows authentication using supported gesture/biometric/PIN mechanisms."],["Key trust","A trust design involving keys and identity service prerequisites."]],"mechanism":"Hello for Business provisions an organizational credential. A user gesture unlocks use of key material; it is not a reusable network password. Deployment depends on supported identity/trust, device and policy requirements.","prereq":"Confirm supported trust model, join state, OS, TPM requirements where applicable, MFA/provisioning prerequisites and policy scope. Validate sign-in with test identity.","scenario":"A user's local convenience PIN is enabled. Is this sufficient evidence of Windows Hello for Business provisioning?","choices":["No; verify organizational credential provisioning and applicable trust/sign-in evidence.","Yes, any PIN proves organizational key trust","Reset the user's password to configure Hello"],"why":["Hello for Business provisions an organizational credential. A user gesture unlocks use of key material; it is not a reusable network password. Deployment depends on supported identity/trust, device and policy requirements.","A local PIN and organizational Hello credential are distinct.","Password reset does not implement the required trust or policy."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/security/identity-protection/hello-for-business/"},{"id":"INF-017","terms":[["LAPS","Windows Local Administrator Password Solution for managed local administrator password rotation and backup."],["Password backup directory","Authorized AD DS or Entra location holding supported recovery information."]],"mechanism":"Windows LAPS changes a supported local account password and backs it up according to policy. Retrieval permission is separate from local administrator membership. Rotation success and backup success should be verified together without sharing secrets.","prereq":"Confirm supported OS and join, backup directory, account existence, eligible policy and authorized recovery role. Use disposable endpoint only.","scenario":"A local admin password rotated, but an authorized operator cannot find the expected backup record. What should be checked?","choices":["Check backup directory configuration, device identity, policy and retrieval permissions.","Publish the password in a ticket","Use the employee's Entra password as local admin"],"why":["Windows LAPS changes a supported local account password and backs it up according to policy. Retrieval permission is separate from local administrator membership. Rotation success and backup success should be verified together without sharing secrets.","Publishing a secret creates security exposure.","Employee account recovery and managed local admin passwords are distinct."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/device-security/laps/deploy-policy"},{"id":"INF-018","terms":[["Local group","An OS-managed group governing applicable local privileges."],["Membership action","Supported policy operation that adds, removes or replaces membership according to configured behavior."]],"mechanism":"Local group membership controls device-level authorization. Central policy can manage supported local groups; removing or replacing members can lock out support or break other workflows. Effective state must be verified on the endpoint.","prereq":"Confirm supported Windows profile, actual current membership and recovery path. Pilot precise membership changes; inspect local result and an excluded endpoint.","scenario":"The pilot policy should remove one unauthorized local admin but keep other approved admins. What is the safe approach?","choices":["Use a supported narrowly scoped membership change and verify current/effective membership before broader rollout.","Replace every local group with an empty list","Assign all users local Administrator"],"why":["Local group membership controls device-level authorization. Central policy can manage supported local groups; removing or replacing members can lock out support or break other workflows. Effective state must be verified on the endpoint.","Broad replacement can remove recovery access.","Adding everyone violates least privilege."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/device-configuration/endpoint-security/account-protection"}];
window.MD102_OBJECTIVE_DEPTH_R5.push(...[{"id":"MAN-019","terms":[["Classic Autopilot","Windows Autopilot deployment based on eligible registered hardware and deployment profiles."],["Device preparation","Separate supported cloud-native Windows provisioning approach with different setup and targeting behavior."]],"mechanism":"Select the family by required join type, supported modes, device registration/targeting requirements and setup evidence. Both orchestrate the existing supported Windows installation rather than deploying a custom disk image. Device preparation does not support classic hybrid join scenarios.","prereq":"Confirm supported Windows version, ownership, tenant enrollment prerequisites, user/device group design and exact supported feature matrix.","scenario":"A project requires supported hybrid Entra join at deployment. Which family fits?","choices":["Evaluate classic Autopilot's documented hybrid deployment path.","Use device preparation regardless of join requirement","Use app configuration alone"],"why":["Select the family by required join type, supported modes, device registration/targeting requirements and setup evidence. Both orchestrate the existing supported Windows installation rather than deploying a custom disk image. Device preparation does not support classic hybrid join scenarios.","Device preparation supports cloud join, not classic hybrid scenarios.","App configuration does not establish organizational identity."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/autopilot/device-preparation/compare"},{"id":"MAN-020","terms":[["User-driven","Setup driven by a user signing in during supported Autopilot provisioning."],["Pre-provisioning","Technician phase stages supported device-targeted workloads before handoff."],["Self-deploying","Supported userless setup scenario with applicable hardware trust prerequisites."]],"mechanism":"Mode selection changes when identity is established, what can be staged and whether a user is required. Self-deploying and pre-provisioning scenarios have specific hardware/attestation requirements. Choose by use case rather than assuming every mode fits every device.","prereq":"Confirm supported join mode, TPM attestation where required, device registration, licensing, network and application targeting. Use authorized hardware or a supported disposable environment.","scenario":"A shared Windows kiosk should provision without a named employee signing in during setup. Which classic mode is most relevant?","choices":["Evaluate self-deploying mode, subject to hardware and supported join prerequisites.","Use user-driven solely because it has a user sign-in","Use personal Entra registration"],"why":["Mode selection changes when identity is established, what can be staged and whether a user is required. Self-deploying and pre-provisioning scenarios have specific hardware/attestation requirements. Choose by use case rather than assuming every mode fits every device.","A required user sign-in contradicts the userless design.","Personal registration is not corporate provisioning."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/autopilot/windows-autopilot"},{"id":"MAN-021","terms":[["Naming template","Supported pattern for generating a device name during applicable provisioning."],["Uniqueness","Requirement to avoid conflicting names in the intended environment."]],"mechanism":"A classic Autopilot profile can apply supported naming templates in applicable join scenarios. Supported characters, length and variables vary by workflow; a device name is only a label, not the stable device ID or policy target.","prereq":"Check current naming restrictions, supported join/mode, assigned profile and actual hostname. Ensure unique naming logic and correlation with directory and managed IDs.","scenario":"Two lab PCs receive identical names under a fixed prefix-only template. What is the best correction?","choices":["Choose supported variable-based or otherwise unique naming design and retest profile assignment.","Delete their management records based on name alone","Use identical name as device identity"],"why":["A classic Autopilot profile can apply supported naming templates in applicable join scenarios. Supported characters, length and variables vary by workflow; a device name is only a label, not the stable device ID or policy target.","Names are not stable unique identifiers; deleting records risks unrelated assets.","Identical display names cannot establish identity equivalence."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/autopilot/profiles"},{"id":"MAN-022","terms":[["Hardware registration","Classic Autopilot association of eligible hardware identity with the organization."],["OOBE","Out-of-box Windows setup experience where applicable provisioning occurs."]],"mechanism":"Provisioning joins and enrolls the supported device using its assigned workflow, then receives applicable apps and settings. Registration, assignment, directory join, managed record, setup and required app success are separate milestones.","prereq":"Confirm supported hardware and OS, registration/profile, eligible user, network endpoints and device scope. Use disposable hardware, collect setup and management evidence.","scenario":"A classic Autopilot device shows an assigned profile but setup fails before the managed-device record exists. What should be investigated first?","choices":["Verify the profile, network/service access, identity/enrollment prerequisites and setup diagnostics.","Treat assigned profile as successful deployment","Reinstall every app before enrollment"],"why":["Provisioning joins and enrolls the supported device using its assigned workflow, then receives applicable apps and settings. Registration, assignment, directory join, managed record, setup and required app success are separate milestones.","Assignment is service-side intent, not completed endpoint state.","App installation may not yet be reached."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/autopilot/windows-autopilot"},{"id":"MAN-023","terms":[["ESP","Enrollment Status Page tracking selected enrollment setup activities."],["Blocking app","Required application configured to gate applicable setup experience."]],"mechanism":"ESP tracks selected account/device setup activities, depending on applicable profile and workflow. If a required blocking app fails detection or dependency installation, setup can stall even if identity and enrollment succeeded. Track only necessary apps and diagnose the stage.","prereq":"Verify applicable ESP profile, assignment intent, supported apps and dependencies, IME/install context and timeout strategy. Test with a disposable endpoint.","scenario":"ESP stalls because a required Win32 app exits successfully but detection fails. Which action is targeted?","choices":["Correct the app's actual detection/requirement mismatch and retest the pilot.","Disable every ESP tracking requirement globally","Rotate BitLocker keys"],"why":["ESP tracks selected account/device setup activities, depending on applicable profile and workflow. If a required blocking app fails detection or dependency installation, setup can stall even if identity and enrollment succeeded. Track only necessary apps and diagnose the stage.","Global disable removes safeguards without correcting the app.","Key rotation is unrelated to app detection."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/autopilot/enrollment-status"},{"id":"MAN-024","terms":[["Feature upgrade","Move to a supported Windows feature version."],["Safeguard hold","Windows update protection delaying an update for known compatibility risk."]],"mechanism":"Upgrade readiness depends on hardware, OS, application compatibility, update eligibility and policy. Intune can target supported feature releases, while safeguard holds and update processing affect actual offers. A policy assignment is not an installed OS version.","prereq":"Check Windows 11 requirements, update entitlement and service reachability, pilot cohorts, safeguards and rollback/recovery design. Confirm actual build and report status.","scenario":"A pilot Windows device is not offered the target feature version because of a documented safeguard hold. What is safest?","choices":["Investigate compatibility and follow supported mitigation or hold guidance before broader rollout.","Disable safeguard holds for all devices immediately","Mark the upgrade complete because policy exists"],"why":["Upgrade readiness depends on hardware, OS, application compatibility, update eligibility and policy. Intune can target supported feature releases, while safeguard holds and update processing affect actual offers. A policy assignment is not an installed OS version.","Broad removal can expose incompatible endpoints.","Assigned policy is not installation evidence."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/device-updates/windows"},{"id":"MAN-025","terms":[["Cloud PC","Windows 365 hosted Windows endpoint assigned under eligible service licensing."],["Provisioning policy","Configuration defining supported Cloud PC provisioning and connectivity/image choices."],["Azure network connection","Supported customer-network integration option with its own requirements."]],"mechanism":"Windows 365 provisioning allocates a hosted endpoint according to eligible license and provisioning configuration. Image choice, region/network design, identity and access affect whether it provisions and whether the user can connect. A Cloud PC is not simply a locally installed VM.","prereq":"Verify eligible Windows 365 license, user assignment, supported join/network/image options and cost boundary. Design-only unless an authorized test entitlement already exists.","scenario":"A Cloud PC remains provisioning failed and the customer-network health checks fail. What should be examined?","choices":["Verify the applicable network connection health, identity/DNS/connectivity requirements and provisioning details.","Assign a mobile app protection policy to fix networking","Buy more licenses without diagnosing"],"why":["Windows 365 provisioning allocates a hosted endpoint according to eligible license and provisioning configuration. Image choice, region/network design, identity and access affect whether it provisions and whether the user can connect. A Cloud PC is not simply a locally installed VM.","APP does not configure Cloud PC provisioning network.","Licensing is distinct from network prerequisites; purchasing may incur cost."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-365/enterprise/overview"},{"id":"MAN-026","terms":[["Windows Backup for Organizations","Supported Windows settings backup and restore experience for eligible organizational devices."],["Restore","Reapplying supported previously backed-up settings under documented identity and platform prerequisites."]],"mechanism":"Backup and restore has a defined coverage boundary; it can preserve supported settings but does not constitute comprehensive enterprise backup of every file, application and data source. Restore depends on identity and supported device/setup flow.","prereq":"Confirm Windows version, organizational identity, configured policy, documented backup scope and separate file backup strategy. Verify supported settings on an authorized test device.","scenario":"A team expects Windows Backup to replace all file and application disaster recovery. Is that supported?","choices":["No; verify the documented settings scope and retain a separate data/application recovery plan.","Yes, every disk sector is restored","Delete all existing backups"],"why":["Backup and restore has a defined coverage boundary; it can preserve supported settings but does not constitute comprehensive enterprise backup of every file, application and data source. Restore depends on identity and supported device/setup flow.","Settings restore is not a full disk-image guarantee.","Deleting separate recovery mechanisms can leave data unprotected."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/configuration/windows-backup/"},{"id":"MAN-027","terms":[["Settings Catalog","Supported configuration settings grouped by platform/workload."],["CSP","Configuration Service Provider exposing applicable Windows management settings."],["Group Policy analytics","Assessment of imported GPO settings and applicable MDM support."]],"mechanism":"Intune policy expresses desired state via supported OS management interfaces. Group Policy analytics identifies support for migration; it does not guarantee one-to-one equivalence. Imported ADMX requires supported schema/dependencies. Resolve overlapping management authorities and inspect actual state.","prereq":"Use supported Windows edition/version and setting; inspect GPO analytics, CSP/ADMX prerequisites, assignment and local configuration. Pilot harmless settings first.","scenario":"A GPO analytics report identifies an unsupported setting. What should IT do?","choices":["Investigate supported alternative or retain required legacy management until a validated migration exists.","Force every GPO registry value through an arbitrary OMA-URI","Remove the legacy control immediately"],"why":["Intune policy expresses desired state via supported OS management interfaces. Group Policy analytics identifies support for migration; it does not guarantee one-to-one equivalence. Imported ADMX requires supported schema/dependencies. Resolve overlapping management authorities and inspect actual state.","Arbitrary URI does not establish a supported CSP mapping.","Removing control before a replacement is verified can create risk."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/device-configuration/import-group-policy-analytics"},{"id":"MAN-028","terms":[["Android management mode","Enrollment mode governing which Android Enterprise policy surfaces apply."],["Configuration profile","Collection of supported platform settings and assignments."]],"mechanism":"Android profiles are interpreted according to device mode, supported OS and OEM capabilities. A fully managed corporate device and personal work profile have different management boundaries. Inspect actual scope and local behavior rather than assuming every setting is available.","prereq":"Confirm mode, OS/OEM, supported profile type and approved scope; compare one included and one excluded endpoint.","scenario":"A restriction is unavailable for personally owned work-profile devices but exists for fully managed devices. What should IT do?","choices":["Review supported management mode and choose a policy consistent with ownership and business requirements.","Apply the fully managed profile and assume it will work","Force personal devices into corporate mode without consent"],"why":["Android profiles are interpreted according to device mode, supported OS and OEM capabilities. A fully managed corporate device and personal work profile have different management boundaries. Inspect actual scope and local behavior rather than assuming every setting is available.","Unsupported setting may not apply in the selected mode.","Changing ownership/management scope requires a justified authorized design."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/device-configuration/overview"},{"id":"MAN-029","terms":[["Supervised iOS/iPadOS","Apple state enabling additional device management capabilities in supported corporate flows."],["Restriction","A supported platform setting limiting a device or app capability."]],"mechanism":"Apple MDM uses platform-specific supported payloads and communication. Some controls require supervision or particular OS versions. A policy can report not applicable when the endpoint does not support the setting. Personal enrollment must respect privacy boundaries.","prereq":"Confirm supported iOS/iPadOS version, supervision/enrollment type, Apple push connectivity and supported setting. Verify actual behavior and per-setting report.","scenario":"An iOS restriction explicitly requires supervision but the test BYOD phone is unsupervised. What is the appropriate conclusion?","choices":["The requirement is not supported by that enrollment state; choose a privacy-compatible alternative or authorized corporate design.","Repeat sync until supervision appears","Apply Windows CSP instead"],"why":["Apple MDM uses platform-specific supported payloads and communication. Some controls require supervision or particular OS versions. A policy can report not applicable when the endpoint does not support the setting. Personal enrollment must respect privacy boundaries.","Sync does not change device supervision state.","Windows CSP is not Apple MDM configuration."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/device-configuration/overview"},{"id":"MAN-030","terms":[["macOS MDM channel","Supported macOS device or user management delivery context."],["Payload","Platform-specific configuration data interpreted by Apple management mechanisms."]],"mechanism":"macOS settings, extensions and approvals have Apple-specific capabilities and OS prerequisites. Device enrollment and user channel can influence delivery. Policy report alone is insufficient for settings that require user approval or local state evidence.","prereq":"Check enrollment method, OS, APNs, supported payload/approval requirements, assignment and local evidence. Avoid changing an everyday workstation for testing.","scenario":"An approved system extension does not become active on a lab Mac. What should be investigated?","choices":["Check the supported macOS payload, OS and extension/approval prerequisites plus assignment and local logs.","Create a Windows registry policy","Assume successful profile save means active extension"],"why":["macOS settings, extensions and approvals have Apple-specific capabilities and OS prerequisites. Device enrollment and user channel can influence delivery. Policy report alone is insufficient for settings that require user approval or local state evidence.","Windows registry configuration is the wrong platform.","A saved profile is desired state, not local extension activation."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/device-configuration/overview"},{"id":"MAN-031","terms":[["Specialty device","Supported purpose-specific endpoint with a distinct platform or management surface."],["Support matrix","Current list of eligible device/OS features and configuration limits."]],"mechanism":"Teams Rooms, HoloLens and Zebra scenarios can differ in OS, enrollment, identity, user interaction and supported settings. Select controls based on documented hardware/OS management capability rather than device display name or standard laptop assumptions.","prereq":"Identify exact supported platform and lifecycle status, enrollment mode, current documentation and business use. Validate only supported pilot controls.","scenario":"A specialty scanner does not support a standard Windows laptop setting. What is best?","choices":["Consult the model/platform support matrix and use supported management configuration.","Force the standard Windows profile","Enroll all specialty devices in personal iOS mode"],"why":["Teams Rooms, HoloLens and Zebra scenarios can differ in OS, enrollment, identity, user interaction and supported settings. Select controls based on documented hardware/OS management capability rather than device display name or standard laptop assumptions.","Unsupported Windows setting may not exist on the scanner platform.","iOS enrollment is unrelated to the specified hardware."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/ref-supported-platforms"},{"id":"MAN-032","terms":[["Assignment filter","Supported condition refining an applicable Intune assignment by device attributes."],["Enrollment time grouping","Supported group placement during enrollment for eligible provisioning flows."]],"mechanism":"Entra group membership defines the base target; Intune filters refine supported assignments; enrollment time grouping addresses supported early group placement in specific flows. Include/exclude logic and evaluation latency are not interchangeable.","prereq":"Check supported filter properties and assignment type; test expected inclusion/exclusion. Verify group membership and report data separately.","scenario":"A group contains corporate and personal Windows devices; a supported policy should reach corporate devices only. What fits?","choices":["Use a supported ownership filter to refine that group assignment and confirm both populations.","Use a scope tag as policy targeting","Rename all personal endpoints"],"why":["Entra group membership defines the base target; Intune filters refine supported assignments; enrollment time grouping addresses supported early group placement in specific flows. Include/exclude logic and evaluation latency are not interchangeable.","Scope tags manage administrative visibility.","Names do not implement reliable ownership targeting."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/filters/overview"},{"id":"MAN-033","terms":[["EPM","Endpoint Privilege Management permitting supported elevation under scoped rules."],["Elevation rule","Policy describing eligible executable matching and permitted elevation behavior."]],"mechanism":"EPM separates everyday standard-user access from specific authorized elevated execution. Rules can depend on supported hash, certificate and metadata conditions, child process behavior and approval design. Broad rules can overgrant privileges.","prereq":"Confirm eligible EPM entitlement, supported Windows/agent, least-privilege roles, file identity and rule scope. Pilot reporting before narrowly scoped rules.","scenario":"A vendor signs many unrelated executables with the same certificate. IT needs elevation for one tool only. What is safer?","choices":["Choose a narrowly matched supported rule and verify actual file identity and child-process behavior.","Allow every file signed by that vendor without review","Make users permanent local administrators"],"why":["EPM separates everyday standard-user access from specific authorized elevated execution. Rules can depend on supported hash, certificate and metadata conditions, child process behavior and approval design. Broad rules can overgrant privileges.","A broad certificate rule can elevate unrelated vendor software.","Permanent admin violates the stated least-privilege objective."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/epm/deployment-planning"},{"id":"MAN-034","terms":[["Enterprise App Catalog","Licensed catalog of supported enterprise app packages for Intune management."],["Catalog metadata","Publisher/package/version and supported deployment information supplied for the app."]],"mechanism":"Catalog-supported apps reduce packaging effort, but administrators still choose assignment, requirements, supported update behavior and evidence. Catalog availability does not mean every app is installed or updates automatically without applicable configuration.","prereq":"Confirm supported Catalog entitlement, application availability, version/assignment and current update feature behavior. No new premium license without authorization.","scenario":"A catalog app is visible but not installed on the pilot group. What should be checked?","choices":["Verify deployment assignment, applicability and per-device installation/detection evidence.","Assume catalog visibility deploys apps","Enable an unrelated VPN gateway"],"why":["Catalog-supported apps reduce packaging effort, but administrators still choose assignment, requirements, supported update behavior and evidence. Catalog availability does not mean every app is installed or updates automatically without applicable configuration.","Availability is not an application assignment.","VPN does not establish catalog deployment."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/app-management/deployment/add-enterprise-catalog-app"},{"id":"MAN-035","terms":[["Remote Help","Licensed remote assistance capability with supported identity, role and session controls."],["Consent","Applicable acknowledgement/authorization for supported assistance mode."]],"mechanism":"Remote assistance requires supported clients, service connectivity, eligible entitlement and scoped permissions. Attended and unattended capabilities depend on platform and configuration. Session visibility and audit are distinct from general administrative permissions.","prereq":"Confirm current supported platform/mode, helper and sharer entitlement, role and connectivity. Use only explicitly authorized lab sessions; verify audit evidence.","scenario":"A helpdesk technician lacks permission to remotely control an authorized test endpoint. What is safest?","choices":["Review supported Remote Help role, scope, license and session/consent mode.","Grant unrestricted Global Administrator","Use another user's credentials"],"why":["Remote assistance requires supported clients, service connectivity, eligible entitlement and scoped permissions. Attended and unattended capabilities depend on platform and configuration. Session visibility and audit are distinct from general administrative permissions.","Broad tenant rights exceed the assistance task.","Credential sharing is not an authorized support workflow."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/remote-help/"},{"id":"MAN-036","terms":[["PKI","Public key infrastructure: trust, certificates, issuance and lifecycle management."],["CA","Certificate authority signing supported certificates."],["SCEP","Supported certificate enrollment protocol used in applicable managed certificate profiles."]],"mechanism":"Cloud PKI establishes trusted CA relationships and supports certificate lifecycle for documented managed device scenarios. Root trust and issued client certificate are different artifacts. Successful issuance requires profile targeting, trust chain and valid certificate lifecycle.","prereq":"Confirm entitlement, CA hierarchy, trusted certificate and supported SCEP profile, target devices and issuance/renewal process. Verify metadata without exposing private keys.","scenario":"A client has a root trust certificate but lacks the expected client authentication certificate. What is missing?","choices":["Investigate the issuing CA and applicable client certificate enrollment profile/issuance.","Assume root trust is the client credential","Export and share private keys"],"why":["Cloud PKI establishes trusted CA relationships and supports certificate lifecycle for documented managed device scenarios. Root trust and issued client certificate are different artifacts. Successful issuance requires profile targeting, trust chain and valid certificate lifecycle.","Root trust validates a chain; it does not prove client credential issuance.","Private key sharing defeats credential protection."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/cloud-pki/fundamentals"},{"id":"MAN-037","terms":[["Microsoft Tunnel Gateway","Supported gateway for private-resource connectivity in applicable Intune scenarios."],["Tunnel for MAM","Supported app-focused private connectivity for eligible managed apps on applicable devices."]],"mechanism":"Tunnel for MAM extends supported private access to selected app scenarios without automatically creating device-wide MDM/VPN coverage. Gateway reachability, TLS, app configuration/protection and identity must align.","prereq":"Verify eligible license, supported app/platform, gateway deployment and certificate/network requirements. Design-only unless an existing authorized lab gateway is available.","scenario":"A personal mobile device needs a supported managed app to reach a private service, but all personal app traffic should remain outside the tunnel. Which design fits?","choices":["Evaluate Microsoft Tunnel for MAM with scoped supported app configuration.","Route every personal app through an unrestricted VPN","Use a Windows update ring"],"why":["Tunnel for MAM extends supported private access to selected app scenarios without automatically creating device-wide MDM/VPN coverage. Gateway reachability, TLS, app configuration/protection and identity must align.","Device-wide routing exceeds the specified app boundary.","Updates do not provide private connectivity."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/device-security/microsoft-tunnel/overview"},{"id":"MAN-038","terms":[["Anomaly","Observed metric deviating from a baseline or cohort expectation."],["Proactive insight","Service-generated indicator requiring corroboration and context before action."]],"mechanism":"Advanced Analytics augments endpoint evidence with supported cohort and anomaly insights. A risk recommendation is not proof of root cause. Assess sample size, collection time, denominator and recent change before proposing a pilot.","prereq":"Confirm eligible entitlement, data collection, cohort and time window. Verify against underlying reports and controlled change rather than auto-enforcement.","scenario":"An insight indicates slower startup for one small cohort after a policy change. What is appropriate?","choices":["Compare cohort, timestamps, startup metrics and other changes before testing a bounded fix.","Revert every tenant policy immediately","Treat the insight as confirmed causation"],"why":["Advanced Analytics augments endpoint evidence with supported cohort and anomaly insights. A risk recommendation is not proof of root cause. Assess sample size, collection time, denominator and recent change before proposing a pilot.","Broad rollback may affect unrelated endpoints.","Correlation is not enough to establish root cause."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/endpoint-analytics/"},{"id":"MAN-039","terms":[["Sync","Request for applicable device management check-in/processing."],["Retire","Supported removal of management/work data with platform-specific effects."],["Wipe","Potentially destructive reset/removal action with documented platform behavior."]],"mechanism":"Remote action intent and actual execution are asynchronous. Sync requests check-in; restart affects availability; retire and wipe have distinct data and record effects. Platform and connectivity determine what is supported and when action is processed.","prereq":"Verify ownership, platform, exact authorized target and action effect; record state, approval and backup where necessary. Never practice destructive actions on real endpoints.","scenario":"An offline endpoint needs new policy and no data should be removed. What is the appropriate action?","choices":["Restore connectivity and use supported Sync, then verify processing.","Wipe the endpoint","Retire the endpoint"],"why":["Remote action intent and actual execution are asynchronous. Sync requests check-in; restart affects availability; retire and wipe have distinct data and record effects. Platform and connectivity determine what is supported and when action is processed.","Wipe may destroy data and exceeds the requirement.","Retire removes management rather than requesting policy check-in."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/device-management/actions/retire"},{"id":"MAN-040","terms":[["Bulk action","Administrative operation submitted for multiple selected endpoints."],["Blast radius","Extent of affected systems/data if a change is wrong."]],"mechanism":"Bulk actions amplify both useful operations and mistakes. Eligibility, platform, exact selected population, permissions and action effects must be evaluated per device. Partial success and pending execution require follow-up.","prereq":"Preview exact device set and count, test individual action, obtain approval and document per-device result. Avoid bulk destructive operations in real systems.","scenario":"A bulk restart query accidentally includes production kiosks during peak hours. What is appropriate?","choices":["Do not submit; correct the filtered selection and verify affected devices and maintenance approval.","Submit and assume filters correct themselves","Use bulk wipe instead"],"why":["Bulk actions amplify both useful operations and mistakes. Eligibility, platform, exact selected population, permissions and action effects must be evaluated per device. Partial success and pending execution require follow-up.","Selection errors do not automatically resolve after submission.","Wipe is more destructive and unrelated to the restart request."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/device-management/actions/"},{"id":"MAN-041","terms":[["Security intelligence","Defender Antivirus threat definitions and related supported security updates."],["Signature freshness","Age/version of installed detection intelligence."]],"mechanism":"An antivirus intelligence update refreshes detection content, distinct from OS feature updates and Defender EDR onboarding. A remote action request does not prove the local update succeeded; correlate device state and reporting.","prereq":"Confirm supported Defender Antivirus configuration and device connectivity, authorized action and report. Verify actual signature version/timestamp on lab endpoint.","scenario":"A laptop has outdated AV definitions but its OS is fully patched. What should IT evaluate?","choices":["Update Defender security intelligence and verify the installed version.","Deploy a Windows feature upgrade solely to change AV signatures","Rotate local admin password"],"why":["An antivirus intelligence update refreshes detection content, distinct from OS feature updates and Defender EDR onboarding. A remote action request does not prove the local update succeeded; correlate device state and reporting.","Feature version is not the same as security intelligence.","Password rotation does not update malware definitions."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/device-management/actions/"},{"id":"MAN-042","terms":[["Recovery key rotation","Generating new supported BitLocker recovery information and backing it up according to the configured workflow."],["Escrow","Secure authorized storage of recovery metadata and secret."]],"mechanism":"Rotation changes recovery material, not the underlying requirement for volume encryption. Verify both successful backup and appropriate access to the new matching protector before treating recovery as ready. Never expose the key itself.","prereq":"Use authorized disposable Windows endpoint with supported encryption, join and recovery backup prerequisites; confirm role and recovery plan. Record only redacted metadata.","scenario":"A key rotation action appears submitted but new recovery information is not confirmed. What should IT do?","choices":["Inspect device action status, check-in and matching recovery backup metadata; do not claim recovery readiness yet.","Publish old and new keys in a ticket","Assume submission proves successful escrow"],"why":["Rotation changes recovery material, not the underlying requirement for volume encryption. Verify both successful backup and appropriate access to the new matching protector before treating recovery as ready. Never expose the key itself.","Recovery secrets should remain restricted.","Asynchronous submission is not proof of successful backup."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/device-configuration/endpoint-security/encrypt-bitlocker-windows"},{"id":"MAN-043","terms":[["LAPS rotation","Change of the managed supported local account password under applicable LAPS policy."],["Rotation timestamp","Evidence of a managed password change, separate from password disclosure."]],"mechanism":"Intune may request supported remote LAPS rotation. The endpoint must process it and back up the resulting password to the configured directory. It does not reset the user's cloud password.","prereq":"Verify supported Windows LAPS policy, local account and backup configuration, appropriate permission and device check-in. Never record actual password in evidence.","scenario":"The requested local password rotation is pending on an offline PC. What proves completion?","choices":["Fresh device processing and authorized directory backup metadata showing the intended rotation.","The admin center button was clicked","Reset the employee's Entra password"],"why":["Intune may request supported remote LAPS rotation. The endpoint must process it and back up the resulting password to the configured directory. It does not reset the user's cloud password.","Submission is only control-plane intent.","Employee cloud password is a different credential."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/device-security/laps/overview"},{"id":"MAN-044","terms":[["KQL","Kusto Query Language used to query supported data schemas."],["Projection","Selecting returned fields in an applicable query."],["Filter","Limiting returned records using a supported predicate."]],"mechanism":"Intune device query uses supported KQL-like query surfaces and schemas; it is not general arbitrary SQL or Microsoft Graph filtering. Query a bounded device/population, select relevant fields and interpret freshness/availability.","prereq":"Confirm current entitlement, supported device/query type, RBAC and schema. Use read-only query; redact identifiers and validate returned field meaning.","scenario":"A device query returns no rows after filtering a nonexistent field. What should be checked?","choices":["Verify the supported query schema, field names, scope and syntax.","Request tenantwide write permission","Wipe the device to populate rows"],"why":["Intune device query uses supported KQL-like query surfaces and schemas; it is not general arbitrary SQL or Microsoft Graph filtering. Query a bounded device/population, select relevant fields and interpret freshness/availability.","Write permissions do not fix a read-only schema mismatch.","Wipe is destructive and unrelated to query syntax."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/endpoint-analytics/"},{"id":"MAN-045","terms":[["Diagnostics collection","Supported retrieval of device diagnostic logs for investigation."],["Correlation","Matching timestamps, device/user context and events across evidence sources."]],"mechanism":"Troubleshooting uses the appropriate blade/report, target user/device assignment and local logs. Diagnostics may be delayed or contain sensitive information. Use smallest needed data, compare timestamps and verify the specific component that failed.","prereq":"Confirm authorization and supported collection mode. Inspect applicable app/MDM logs and user assignments; redact customer data and secrets.","scenario":"A required app fails for one user while others succeed. Which evidence is most useful?","choices":["Compare that user's assignments and device requirements, install/detection logs and exact failure timestamp.","Wipe the device before collecting evidence","Disable all app policies"],"why":["Troubleshooting uses the appropriate blade/report, target user/device assignment and local logs. Diagnostics may be delayed or contain sensitive information. Use smallest needed data, compare timestamps and verify the specific component that failed.","Wipe destroys diagnostic evidence and data.","Broad disable affects unrelated users and obscures the cause."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/device-management/actions/collect-diagnostics"}]);
window.MD102_OBJECTIVE_DEPTH_R5.push(...[{"id":"SEC-046","terms":[["Antivirus","Software controls that detect and prevent applicable malicious content or behavior."],["Real-time protection","Supported continuous inspection while processes/files are used."],["Exclusion","Configured exception to applicable detection or scanning."]],"mechanism":"Endpoint security antivirus policy configures supported Defender settings. Real-time protection, scanning schedules, intelligence, cloud-delivered features and exclusions affect different behaviors; EDR onboarding is a separate workload. Evaluate local service status as well as policy reports.","prereq":"Confirm supported OS and antivirus mode, management authority, applicable settings and existing security agent interactions. Pilot a narrowly scoped benign test; avoid malicious files.","scenario":"An app installer fails because an AV control detects a suspicious file. What is the first response?","choices":["Investigate the detection and approved publisher/file evidence, then use only justified scoped configuration if necessary.","Disable antivirus across all devices","Add a blanket exclusion for the system drive"],"why":["Endpoint security antivirus policy configures supported Defender settings. Real-time protection, scanning schedules, intelligence, cloud-delivered features and exclusions affect different behaviors; EDR onboarding is a separate workload. Evaluate local service status as well as policy reports.","Broad disable removes protection without validating the detection.","A drive-wide exclusion hides unrelated risks."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/device-security/endpoint-security-policies"},{"id":"SEC-047","terms":[["Volume encryption","Cryptographic protection for data stored on an applicable disk volume."],["TPM","Trusted Platform Module supporting protected cryptographic operations."],["Recovery escrow","Authorized backup of recovery information, distinct from active encryption."]],"mechanism":"BitLocker encryption and protection state, key protectors, recovery information backup and compliance assessment are separate facts. An endpoint may be encrypted but have missing recovery metadata. A policy cannot be considered operationally successful until recovery readiness is verified.","prereq":"Use authorized disposable endpoint, supported OS/TPM and encryption policy. Confirm recovery backup destination and recovery access; never include actual recovery secret in evidence.","scenario":"The portal reports encrypted, but the expected recovery protector record is missing. What should block expansion?","choices":["Validate matching device/protector metadata, authorized recovery backup and remediation of the escrow issue.","Assume encryption guarantees recovery","Publish recovery keys in screenshots"],"why":["BitLocker encryption and protection state, key protectors, recovery information backup and compliance assessment are separate facts. An endpoint may be encrypted but have missing recovery metadata. A policy cannot be considered operationally successful until recovery readiness is verified.","Encryption does not prove successful backup.","Recovery secrets must never be published."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/device-configuration/endpoint-security/encrypt-bitlocker-windows"},{"id":"SEC-048","terms":[["Firewall profile","Set of network firewall settings applying to a supported network category."],["Inbound rule","Supported condition allowing or blocking traffic arriving at a device."],["Protocol and port","Communication type and logical endpoint used to match applicable network traffic."]],"mechanism":"A firewall evaluates applicable traffic against profile settings and rules. Scope, direction, port/protocol, address and program constraints can determine whether legitimate traffic works. Firewall control is not application detection or device encryption.","prereq":"Identify specific approved flow and actual network profile; use narrow pilot rule with supported settings. Verify allowed traffic and unrelated traffic still blocked.","scenario":"A service needs TCP access on one port on a private network profile only. What is the best approach?","choices":["Configure a narrow supported inbound rule matching the required port, protocol and profile; validate positive/negative traffic.","Disable all Windows firewall profiles","Add an unrelated Defender scan exclusion"],"why":["A firewall evaluates applicable traffic against profile settings and rules. Scope, direction, port/protocol, address and program constraints can determine whether legitimate traffic works. Firewall control is not application detection or device encryption.","Broad disable allows unrelated traffic.","AV scanning exclusions do not configure inbound firewall behavior."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/device-configuration/endpoint-security/firewall"},{"id":"SEC-049","terms":[["ASR","Attack surface reduction rules targeting applicable risky actions."],["Audit mode","Supported mode logging applicable events without that rule's blocking enforcement."],["Block mode","Supported mode enforcing prevention for the applicable rule."]],"mechanism":"ASR can prevent documented behaviors beyond ordinary malware signatures. Rules have specific requirements, modes and telemetry. Audit-first evaluation helps distinguish legitimate workload impact from risk, but audit is not enforcement. Exceptions require narrow justification.","prereq":"Check supported rule and Defender state, policy source, licensing/OS and applicable audit telemetry. Pilot then verify enforcement and impact before broad rollout.","scenario":"A new ASR rule may interrupt an approved line-of-business process. How should the rollout begin?","choices":["Apply supported audit mode to a small pilot and review actual telemetry and workload compatibility.","Enforce all rules tenantwide immediately","Disable Defender permanently"],"why":["ASR can prevent documented behaviors beyond ordinary malware signatures. Rules have specific requirements, modes and telemetry. Audit-first evaluation helps distinguish legitimate workload impact from risk, but audit is not enforcement. Exceptions require narrow justification.","Broad untested blocks can interrupt business operations.","Disabling Defender sacrifices protection without evaluating the specific rule."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/defender-endpoint/attack-surface-reduction-rules-configure"},{"id":"SEC-050","terms":[["Security baseline","Microsoft-provided recommended settings collection for supported workload/platform."],["Settings overlap","Two management policies configuring the same or related settings."],["Effective state","Actual settings in effect on the endpoint after applicable processing."]],"mechanism":"A baseline accelerates standardized security design, but organization requirements and existing policies may differ. Baseline version changes can add or modify settings; conflicts are resolved by examining individual supported settings and management authority, not assuming latest policy always wins.","prereq":"Review current baseline version and per-setting behavior, inventory overlapping settings and target a pilot. Verify actual local controls and report results; document justified exceptions.","scenario":"A custom firewall profile and baseline specify incompatible behavior. What should the administrator do?","choices":["Inspect affected settings, policy scope and documented behavior; reconcile intended settings before retest.","Assume the newest baseline always overrides every other profile","Assign both to every device"],"why":["A baseline accelerates standardized security design, but organization requirements and existing policies may differ. Baseline version changes can add or modify settings; conflicts are resolved by examining individual supported settings and management authority, not assuming latest policy always wins.","Precedence depends on specific workload/setting, not universal creation time.","Expanding conflict increases impact."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/device-security/endpoint-security-policies"},{"id":"SEC-051","terms":[["MDE","Microsoft Defender for Endpoint security service."],["EDR","Endpoint detection and response, using sensor telemetry and investigation capabilities."],["Risk signal","Applicable endpoint risk information shared with supported management/access workflows."]],"mechanism":"Supported Intune-MDE integration connects endpoint management with security posture. EDR onboarding establishes the sensor relationship; connector configuration enables supported sharing; a compliance rule can consume applicable risk signals. Incidents need separate triage and corroboration.","prereq":"Confirm eligible MDE/Intune licensing, supported device, connector, onboarding and permissions. Verify Defender device evidence and applicable Intune risk/compliance result.","scenario":"An Intune-managed device reports antivirus healthy but no MDE risk signal. What should be checked?","choices":["Check MDE onboarding, service connection and applicable risk integration independently of antivirus health.","Assume antivirus health proves EDR onboarding","Reassign an unrelated app"],"why":["Supported Intune-MDE integration connects endpoint management with security posture. EDR onboarding establishes the sensor relationship; connector configuration enables supported sharing; a compliance rule can consume applicable risk signals. Incidents need separate triage and corroboration.","Antivirus protection and EDR sensor enrollment are different.","App assignment does not establish MDE integration."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/device-security/microsoft-defender/overview"},{"id":"SEC-052","terms":[["Onboarding package","Supported configuration establishing an endpoint's relationship with Defender for Endpoint."],["Sensor","Endpoint component collecting applicable detection/response telemetry."]],"mechanism":"Onboarding makes an eligible endpoint visible and monitored in MDE through a supported method. Intune enrollment and MDE onboarding are independent relationships. Device visibility, sensor health and telemetry freshness must be inspected; do not create harmful security test activity.","prereq":"Confirm supported OS/license, onboarding method and required service connectivity. Use authorized lab endpoint and benign supported validation; inspect device sensor and record.","scenario":"An enrolled Windows device is absent from Defender's inventory. Which state is relevant?","choices":["Verify the MDE onboarding policy, sensor status and service connectivity for the exact endpoint.","Change the MDM enrollment display name","Assume all Intune devices are automatically onboarded"],"why":["Onboarding makes an eligible endpoint visible and monitored in MDE through a supported method. Intune enrollment and MDE onboarding are independent relationships. Device visibility, sensor health and telemetry freshness must be inspected; do not create harmful security test activity.","Display name does not establish security service connection.","MDM management and MDE onboarding are separate."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/defender-endpoint/onboarding-endpoint-manager"},{"id":"SEC-053","terms":[["App Control for Business","Supported Windows policy controlling code execution according to trust rules."],["Signer rule","Trust decision based on supported signing identity conditions."],["Audit event","Evidence of what would be affected by an applicable control in audit mode."]],"mechanism":"App Control makes allow/trust decisions for applicable code execution; app installation management and antivirus serve different functions. A restrictive policy can block required code if trust rules are incomplete. Audit and pilot evidence help refine before enforcement.","prereq":"Check supported Windows platform/policy format, signer trust and app inventory; use audit mode and authorized benign workloads. Verify events and rollback strategy.","scenario":"A business tool is unsigned and required on selected endpoints. What should precede enforcement of a restrictive app-control policy?","choices":["Review supported trust rules, audit events, approval and controlled pilot compatibility.","Deploy deny policy to every endpoint immediately","Treat a Win32 required assignment as sufficient execution authorization"],"why":["App Control makes allow/trust decisions for applicable code execution; app installation management and antivirus serve different functions. A restrictive policy can block required code if trust rules are incomplete. Audit and pilot evidence help refine before enforcement.","Untested enforcement can block needed applications.","Installation intent does not override code-execution control."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/device-security/endpoint-security-policies"},{"id":"SEC-054","terms":[["Update ring","Cohort or policy design for staged update behavior."],["Maintenance window","Approved time window for applicable update/restart activity."],["Rollback criterion","Predefined evidence threshold prompting containment or recovery."]],"mechanism":"Plan updates around risk, hardware and application compatibility, service requirements and criticality. Separate initial test, pilot and broad cohorts. Windows, Apple and Android support different servicing mechanisms; monitoring and rollback need platform-specific evidence.","prereq":"Inventory platforms/builds and support lifecycle, maintenance needs, update eligibility and rollback constraints. Define cohort counts, success threshold and stop conditions.","scenario":"A new update causes a high-impact application failure in a small pilot. What should happen?","choices":["Contain expansion, gather evidence and follow supported mitigation or rollback criteria.","Expand deployment to collect more affected devices","Disable all security updates indefinitely"],"why":["Plan updates around risk, hardware and application compatibility, service requirements and criticality. Separate initial test, pilot and broad cohorts. Windows, Apple and Android support different servicing mechanisms; monitoring and rollback need platform-specific evidence.","Expansion increases known impact.","Indefinite disable introduces unbounded security risk."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/device-updates/"},{"id":"SEC-055","terms":[["Update ring policy","Configures supported Windows update deferrals, deadlines and restart behavior."],["Feature update policy","Targets a supported Windows feature release."],["Quality update","Servicing update distinct from a major feature-version change."]],"mechanism":"Use the applicable Windows servicing policy type for intended version versus behavior. Update eligibility, holds, deadlines and actual offer/install/restart status affect outcomes; policy creation does not upgrade a PC. Verify support lifecycle of target version.","prereq":"Confirm supported Windows version/edition and service eligibility, assigned groups and other management sources. Pilot, inspect actual build and update reports.","scenario":"The team wants to keep eligible Windows devices at an approved feature version while servicing routine quality updates. Which design fits?","choices":["Use supported feature-target policy with appropriately coordinated update-ring behavior.","Use an app protection policy to target OS version","Rename the update policy and assume devices changed"],"why":["Use the applicable Windows servicing policy type for intended version versus behavior. Update eligibility, holds, deadlines and actual offer/install/restart status affect outcomes; policy creation does not upgrade a PC. Verify support lifecycle of target version.","App protection does not configure Windows servicing.","Names are not device update evidence."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/device-updates/windows/"},{"id":"SEC-056","terms":[["Windows Autopatch","Microsoft-managed supported update orchestration for eligible organizations and endpoints."],["Hotpatch","Supported servicing mechanism reducing certain restart requirements on eligible devices."],["Eligibility","Documented license, platform and configuration conditions required by a feature."]],"mechanism":"Autopatch orchestrates applicable Windows servicing workflows; Hotpatch is a specific supported update approach and does not eliminate every restart. Eligibility and cadence vary by OS/build, configuration and current service requirements. Confirm actual reporting rather than assuming availability from a menu.","prereq":"Check current license and OS requirements, baseline build, device registration, update policies and restart expectations. Design-only without eligible lab resources.","scenario":"A manager expects every Windows endpoint to receive Hotpatch without restarting ever. What is correct?","choices":["Verify current eligible device/build/license and documented baseline/restart cadence; do not promise zero restarts.","Enable on every device regardless of eligibility","Disable reboot notification and call it Hotpatch"],"why":["Autopatch orchestrates applicable Windows servicing workflows; Hotpatch is a specific supported update approach and does not eliminate every restart. Eligibility and cadence vary by OS/build, configuration and current service requirements. Confirm actual reporting rather than assuming availability from a menu.","Unsupported endpoints cannot gain feature entitlement by assignment.","Notification settings are not Hotpatch servicing."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/device-updates/windows/"},{"id":"SEC-057","terms":[["Apple managed software update","Supported Apple MDM update mechanisms with platform/OS prerequisites."],["Enforcement deadline","Applicable configured time by which an eligible update should occur."]],"mechanism":"Apple OS servicing follows documented management commands/settings and supported OS/supervision requirements. macOS and iOS/iPadOS may have different supported settings and user experience. Version targeting, deferral and enforcement need separate evidence.","prereq":"Verify current supported Apple OS, device enrollment/supervision, Settings Catalog option and connectivity; compare actual version and applicable reporting.","scenario":"A personally enrolled unsupervised iPhone does not support a required supervised update setting. What should IT do?","choices":["Review documented supported controls for that enrollment type and choose an authorized compliant design.","Apply a Windows update ring","Assume repeated sync grants supervision"],"why":["Apple OS servicing follows documented management commands/settings and supported OS/supervision requirements. macOS and iOS/iPadOS may have different supported settings and user experience. Version targeting, deferral and enforcement need separate evidence.","Windows ring is not an Apple update mechanism.","Sync does not change supervision state."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/device-updates/"},{"id":"SEC-058","terms":[["FOTA","Firmware-over-the-air: supported vendor mechanism for remotely distributing firmware updates."],["OEM","Original equipment manufacturer whose device support may govern update capabilities."]],"mechanism":"Android Enterprise system update policy and vendor-specific FOTA features are not identical. Device mode, OEM model, management platform, battery/network and supported policies determine behavior. Firmware support cannot be inferred from Android version alone.","prereq":"Check supported mode, exact OEM/model and current documented FOTA eligibility. Pilot update timing, observe actual version and device health.","scenario":"A firmware feature works on one vendor's dedicated devices but not a different vendor. What should be investigated?","choices":["Check OEM/model eligibility and supported management/update APIs for the second device.","Copy the same vendor profile and assume compatibility","Use macOS update settings"],"why":["Android Enterprise system update policy and vendor-specific FOTA features are not identical. Device mode, OEM model, management platform, battery/network and supported policies determine behavior. Firmware support cannot be inferred from Android version alone.","Vendor-specific APIs may not be portable.","macOS settings do not govern Android firmware."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/device-updates/"},{"id":"SEC-059","terms":[["Delivery Optimization","Supported Windows content-delivery mechanism that can use peers or cache depending on configuration."],["Peer group","Applicable grouping controlling supported peer-sharing behavior."],["Bandwidth limit","Supported control limiting download/upload usage under documented conditions."]],"mechanism":"Delivery Optimization improves applicable content delivery rather than changing update approval. Mode, group, network and cache accessibility determine possible sources. Measure actual traffic/content source and update outcomes, not just configured policy.","prereq":"Check current supported Windows settings and network design; verify privacy/security requirements, source availability and traffic measurements in pilot.","scenario":"A remote branch downloads duplicate update content over constrained WAN links. What capability is relevant?","choices":["Evaluate appropriate Delivery Optimization peer/cache settings and verify actual content-source traffic.","Rotate recovery keys","Disable every update permanently"],"why":["Delivery Optimization improves applicable content delivery rather than changing update approval. Mode, group, network and cache accessibility determine possible sources. Measure actual traffic/content source and update outcomes, not just configured policy.","Key rotation is unrelated to content delivery.","Disabling updates trades availability problem for security exposure."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/deployment/do/waas-delivery-optimization"},{"id":"SEC-060","terms":[["Offer","Update made available to eligible device under supported policy/service rules."],["Installation status","Reported progress/result of applying an update."],["Restart pending","State where installed update may still require completion after restart."]],"mechanism":"Monitoring distinguishes targeted, eligible/offered, downloaded, installed, restart pending and final version. Report latency and safeguard/compatibility holds can affect interpretation. Compare denominators and actual device OS/build.","prereq":"Confirm expected cohort and report scope, last update/check-in, safeguard information and local version evidence. Escalate specific blocked stage rather than declaring all complete.","scenario":"An update report shows 80 installed, 10 pending restart and 10 not offered. Can all 100 be counted complete?","choices":["No; verify each stage, investigate not-offered and pending-restart devices, and confirm final version.","Yes, assigned means installed","Exclude unfinished devices from the denominator without explanation"],"why":["Monitoring distinguishes targeted, eligible/offered, downloaded, installed, restart pending and final version. Report latency and safeguard/compatibility holds can affect interpretation. Compare denominators and actual device OS/build.","Assignment does not prove installation.","Unexplained denominator changes misrepresent deployment health."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/device-updates/"}]);
window.MD102_OBJECTIVE_DEPTH_R5.push(...[{"id":"APP-061","terms":[["Win32 package","Prepared application content for supported Win32 deployment through Intune."],["Install command","Command executed under the configured context to install the application."],["Detection rule","Condition determining whether the app is considered installed."]],"mechanism":"Package creation, applicability requirements, installation execution, return-code interpretation and detection are distinct stages. Define accurate detection and supported execution context from actual install behavior; an installer success exit code does not prove the app is detected.","prereq":"Use licensed/approved benign installer and disposable Windows VM. Verify architecture, requirements, commands, dependency and artifacts before upload/pilot. Plan uninstall.","scenario":"A vendor installer exits 0, but the configured detection file does not exist at the specified path. What should be corrected?","choices":["Compare actual installation artifacts with the detection rule and supported execution context.","Treat installer exit 0 as proof of detected success","Rename installer to .intunewin without preparing content"],"why":["Package creation, applicability requirements, installation execution, return-code interpretation and detection are distinct stages. Define accurate detection and supported execution context from actual install behavior; an installer success exit code does not prove the app is detected.","Detection is independently evaluated.","Renaming does not produce a supported prepared Win32 package."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/app-management/deployment/create-win32-package"},{"id":"APP-062","terms":[["LOB app","Line-of-business app deployed using supported platform/package mechanisms."],["Required assignment","Applicable assignment that requests installation rather than merely offering self-service."],["Available assignment","Applicable assignment allowing eligible users/devices to obtain the app where supported."]],"mechanism":"Application type determines supported content, settings and delivery mechanism. Assignment intent selects intended experience; requirements decide applicability; delivery/installation/detection determine actual result. Compare scope and local evidence, not application visibility alone.","prereq":"Verify supported app type, platform/management state, package or store prerequisites, assignment type and target. Pilot included/excluded devices and local installation.","scenario":"A pilot app must be installed automatically rather than offered in Company Portal only. What assignment is appropriate?","choices":["Use a supported Required assignment to the intended pilot and verify detection/reporting.","Assign Available and assume automatic installation","Add a scope tag instead of assignment"],"why":["Application type determines supported content, settings and delivery mechanism. Assignment intent selects intended experience; requirements decide applicability; delivery/installation/detection determine actual result. Compare scope and local evidence, not application visibility alone.","Available usually offers self-service in supported scenarios, not required installation.","Tags affect administrative visibility, not installation intent."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/app-management/deployment/add-win32"},{"id":"APP-063","terms":[["Quiet Time","Supported app-management policy limiting work-related notifications during configured times."],["Schedule","Configured recurring or date-range time window applicable to the supported policy."]],"mechanism":"Quiet Time is a supported work-notification control for eligible managed mobile app scenarios. It does not power down a phone, block all sign-ins or change OS-wide notification behavior universally. App support and scheduled times determine outcome.","prereq":"Confirm current supported apps, OS and cloud environment; target authorized test user and validate notification behavior inside/outside window.","scenario":"A company wants supported managed Outlook notifications suppressed during nonworking hours, but users may still open work mail. What fits?","choices":["Evaluate Quiet Time policy for supported managed apps and test the schedule.","Block every Microsoft 365 sign-in during evenings","Schedule a device wipe at end of shift"],"why":["Quiet Time is a supported work-notification control for eligible managed mobile app scenarios. It does not power down a phone, block all sign-ins or change OS-wide notification behavior universally. App support and scheduled times determine outcome.","Blocking sign-ins changes access, not just notification behavior.","Wipe is destructive and unrelated."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/app-management/protection/configure-quiet-time"},{"id":"APP-064","terms":[["Microsoft 365 Apps","Subscription-supported Office productivity applications in eligible organizational scenarios."],["Architecture","Application bitness such as x86/x64, with compatibility implications."],["Update channel","Office servicing channel controlling applicable feature release cadence."]],"mechanism":"Office deployment configuration specifies selected apps, supported architecture, channel and other installation choices. Existing Office technology or architecture can create conflicts. License and app installation are separate; verify local apps, activation where applicable and actual channel/build.","prereq":"Check supported OS, existing Office install, applicable subscription entitlement, selected apps/channel and pilot assignment. Confirm user workflow and deployment status.","scenario":"A pilot has an existing conflicting Office installation architecture. What should the team do before deployment?","choices":["Review documented compatibility and supported migration/removal plan, then test intended architecture and channel.","Ignore existing installation and assume every package can coexist","Change only the Windows OS update ring"],"why":["Office deployment configuration specifies selected apps, supported architecture, channel and other installation choices. Existing Office technology or architecture can create conflicts. License and app installation are separate; verify local apps, activation where applicable and actual channel/build.","Incompatible Office installations can fail or disrupt workflows.","OS update rings do not select Office install architecture."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/app-management/deployment/add-microsoft-365-windows"},{"id":"APP-065","terms":[["Office policy","Supported configuration of applicable Microsoft 365 app behaviors."],["Cloud Policy","Supported Microsoft 365 Apps configuration service for eligible user policy scenarios."]],"mechanism":"Microsoft 365 Apps admin center and Intune provide different supported policy/configuration surfaces. Identity, platform, scope and application support determine behavior. Overlapping configuration needs documented workload-specific resolution; an Office policy does not certify device compliance.","prereq":"Confirm Office version/platform, policy support, eligible identity/entitlement and intended configuration source. Test app behavior for included and excluded users.","scenario":"IT wants an Office-specific setting enforced for eligible users, rather than an OS setting. What should be evaluated?","choices":["Use supported Office configuration through the relevant Intune or Microsoft 365 Apps admin center policy surface.","Use BitLocker recovery policy","Assume Windows enrollment alone sets every Office option"],"why":["Microsoft 365 Apps admin center and Intune provide different supported policy/configuration surfaces. Identity, platform, scope and application support determine behavior. Overlapping configuration needs documented workload-specific resolution; an Office policy does not certify device compliance.","Volume encryption does not configure Office app options.","Enrollment creates management relationship, not every app setting."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-365-apps/admin-center/overview"},{"id":"APP-066","terms":[["ODT","Office Deployment Tool used for supported Microsoft 365 Apps installation/configuration workflows."],["ESP tracked apps","Selected required applications relevant to applicable Autopilot setup gating."]],"mechanism":"Office installation during Autopilot depends on deployment method, assignment, setup stage and supported application tracking. Required app dependencies and detection matter; optional availability does not guarantee app readiness before desktop access. Avoid overlapping installers.","prereq":"Check Autopilot scenario, ESP configuration, supported Office delivery method and dependencies; avoid concurrent conflicting Office installation. Test setup and actual Office launch on disposable endpoint.","scenario":"Office must be ready before a user's classic Autopilot setup completes. What must the plan include?","choices":["Supported required deployment and applicable ESP tracking with verified Office installation and dependency behavior.","Mark Office Available only and assume ESP installs it","Use a scope tag as the blocking app"],"why":["Office installation during Autopilot depends on deployment method, assignment, setup stage and supported application tracking. Required app dependencies and detection matter; optional availability does not guarantee app readiness before desktop access. Avoid overlapping installers.","Available assignment does not establish required setup installation.","Tags do not configure ESP application tracking."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/autopilot/enrollment-status"},{"id":"APP-067","terms":[["Servicing profile","Applicable Microsoft 365 Apps management approach for eligible update workflows."],["Installed build","Actual version of Office apps on an endpoint."]],"mechanism":"Microsoft 365 Apps servicing is distinct from Windows OS updates. The admin center can manage supported inventory, configuration and update experiences. Validate current feature support and channel/version policy; reports may lag actual installed builds.","prereq":"Confirm eligible Microsoft 365 Apps deployment, cloud connectivity and supported administration surface. Compare targeted versus actual build/channel and scoped policy result.","scenario":"Windows is up to date but Office apps remain on an old build. What should be checked?","choices":["Office-specific servicing policy, channel, connectivity and actual installed version.","Only the Windows update ring","The device's BitLocker protector"],"why":["Microsoft 365 Apps servicing is distinct from Windows OS updates. The admin center can manage supported inventory, configuration and update experiences. Validate current feature support and channel/version policy; reports may lag actual installed builds.","Windows servicing does not determine every Office update.","Encryption state does not configure Office updates."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-365-apps/admin-center/overview"},{"id":"APP-068","terms":[["Managed Google Play","Android Enterprise app distribution/approval integration for supported managed apps."],["Apps and Books","Apple Business licensing and distribution capability for applicable app acquisition."],["Store token","Supported integration credential with renewal/synchronization requirements."]],"mechanism":"Store integrations connect organizational app acquisition to supported platform management. Assignment, license availability where applicable, integration sync and enrollment mode affect delivery. Android app approvals and Apple licenses are different ecosystems.","prereq":"Confirm authorized platform integration/token, app availability/licensing and eligible management mode. Test pilot assignment and per-device status; renew tokens according to current documentation.","scenario":"A managed Android work profile needs an approved public app. What distribution route is appropriate?","choices":["Managed Google Play through the authorized Intune integration and scoped assignment.","Apple Apps and Books licensing","Windows Win32 content package"],"why":["Store integrations connect organizational app acquisition to supported platform management. Assignment, license availability where applicable, integration sync and enrollment mode affect delivery. Android app approvals and Apple licenses are different ecosystems.","Apple integration does not distribute Android apps.","Win32 package is a Windows app format."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/app-management/deployment/add-managed-google-play"},{"id":"APP-069","terms":[["IME logs","Intune Management Extension logs containing applicable Windows app/script processing evidence."],["Requirement evaluation","Checks whether app is applicable before installation."],["Detection failure","App installation may have executed, but configured presence criteria were not satisfied."]],"mechanism":"Troubleshoot from assignment and eligibility through content delivery, installer execution, exit status and detection. Distinguish not applicable, pending, failed installation and failed detection. A retry is appropriate only after understanding the failing stage.","prereq":"Confirm scoped user/device, supported app type, exact report/error timestamp and relevant logs. Compare actual installation artifacts and execution context, then retest narrow correction.","scenario":"App reports not applicable because the architecture requirement is x64 and a pilot is x86. What should IT do?","choices":["Confirm the requirement is justified and choose an approved supported app/package for the device if needed.","Repeatedly run installer on the incompatible endpoint","Disable every app requirement globally"],"why":["Troubleshoot from assignment and eligibility through content delivery, installer execution, exit status and detection. Distinguish not applicable, pending, failed installation and failed detection. A retry is appropriate only after understanding the failing stage.","Retries do not fix a legitimate applicability mismatch.","Broadly removing requirements risks incompatible deployment."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/app-management/deployment/add-win32"},{"id":"APP-070","terms":[["App protection policy","Supported managed-app policy protecting organizational data and access behavior."],["MAM without enrollment","Supported application management without requiring full device enrollment in applicable scenarios."],["Selective wipe","Removal of applicable organizational app data in supported management scenario."]],"mechanism":"App protection applies to supported apps and identities, controlling applicable data transfer, access and selective removal. It does not make all personal device data managed. Conditional Access can integrate with supported app-protection requirements after the protection design is understood.","prereq":"Check supported platform/app, user eligibility, targeted protection settings and current access requirements. Test work/personal data boundaries with synthetic data only.","scenario":"A contractor needs a supported work app but personal photos must remain outside management. What is appropriate?","choices":["Target supported app protection and validate work-data restrictions; use app-based access controls where required.","Enroll as corporate-owned and wipe personal photos","Use a Windows device firewall profile"],"why":["App protection applies to supported apps and identities, controlling applicable data transfer, access and selective removal. It does not make all personal device data managed. Conditional Access can integrate with supported app-protection requirements after the protection design is understood.","The corporate reset exceeds the stated privacy need.","Windows firewall does not control iOS/Android app data transfer."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/app-management/protection/create-policy"},{"id":"APP-071","terms":[["Require app protection","Supported Conditional Access grant requiring an applicable app protection policy for eligible sign-ins."],["Managed app context","Supported app/account conditions allowing the protection signal to be evaluated."]],"mechanism":"App-based access conditions combine identity sign-in and supported app protection requirements. Design after understanding APP (APP-070) and device compliance access (INF-015). A managed app does not necessarily imply full-device compliance. Review current supported grant and platform behavior before enforcement.","prereq":"Check applicable Entra/Intune entitlement, supported apps/platforms, pilot identities/resources, APP target and emergency exclusions. Use Report-only where supported and inspect sign-in logs.","scenario":"A personal mobile user has a protected Outlook app but no enrolled-device compliance record. Which access design should be evaluated for app-only protection?","choices":["Supported app protection grant, scoped to eligible apps/users, rather than assuming device compliance is required.","Require managed-device compliance regardless of BYOD design","Apply a scope tag to block other apps"],"why":["App-based access conditions combine identity sign-in and supported app protection requirements. Design after understanding APP (APP-070) and device compliance access (INF-015). A managed app does not necessarily imply full-device compliance. Review current supported grant and platform behavior before enforcement.","Device compliance may exclude intended app-only scenario.","Scope tags do not enforce sign-in grants."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/device-security/conditional-access-integration/overview"},{"id":"APP-072","terms":[["App configuration","Delivery of supported app-specific settings through an applicable channel."],["Configuration key","App-defined setting name and expected value/type."],["Delivery channel","Applicable managed-device or managed-app configuration route."]],"mechanism":"App configuration supplies settings such as service URLs or behavior options supported by the application. App protection governs supported organizational data/security behavior. Channel, app support, identity and key schema determine whether settings apply.","prereq":"Confirm supported app-specific keys/types, applicable management channel and scope. Validate actual app behavior and policy report; never place credentials in arbitrary config keys.","scenario":"A mobile app needs a preconfigured corporate URL, while data-copy restrictions are already implemented. What should be added?","choices":["App configuration using the documented supported key and applicable channel.","Replace protection policy with a Windows update ring","Use an app detection rule to set the URL"],"why":["App configuration supplies settings such as service URLs or behavior options supported by the application. App protection governs supported organizational data/security behavior. Channel, app support, identity and key schema determine whether settings apply.","Windows servicing is not app configuration.","Detection verifies presence, not app-specific settings."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/app-management/configuration/overview"}]);
window.MD102_OBJECTIVE_DEPTH_R5.push(...[{"id":"OPE-073","terms":[["Microsoft Graph","API surface for supported Microsoft cloud resources and operations."],["Access token","Authorization artifact accepted by an applicable resource."],["Pagination","Retrieving successive result pages using returned continuation links."],["Throttling","Service rate limiting, commonly indicated by HTTP 429 and applicable retry guidance."]],"mechanism":"Graph automation requests an appropriate resource with supported delegated/application permissions. Process pages until completion; handle authorization, throttling and transient errors; validate outputs. Write operations require separate authorization and reviewed effect. PowerShell is a client/tooling layer, not Graph itself.","prereq":"Use read-only authorized test permission, supported resource and fictional or redacted data. Check API version, permission scope, response schema, paging and retry behavior.","scenario":"An inventory request returns 100 rows and an @odata.nextLink. How should the script handle it?","choices":["Follow each returned nextLink until exhausted, using appropriate authorization and error handling.","Assume the first page is the entire fleet","Request tenantwide write permission to force more rows"],"why":["Graph automation requests an appropriate resource with supported delegated/application permissions. Process pages until completion; handle authorization, throttling and transient errors; validate outputs. Write operations require separate authorization and reviewed effect. PowerShell is a client/tooling layer, not Graph itself.","Server-side paging can return a partial result set.","Write permissions do not solve read-only paging."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/graph/paging"},{"id":"OPE-074","terms":[["Security Copilot","Supported AI-assisted security capabilities subject to current service and entitlement requirements."],["Finding","Generated or reported observation that requires corroboration."],["Device timeline","Chronological endpoint evidence supporting an incident investigation."]],"mechanism":"A supported Security Copilot agent may surface a threat-related finding. Treat generated interpretation as a lead; verify underlying Defender/Intune evidence, affected population, severity, timestamps and authorization before taking action. A recommendation is not proof of threat or change execution.","prereq":"Confirm current feature availability, entitlement and permissions; use fictional event evidence unless explicitly authorized. Check source telemetry and incident response ownership.","scenario":"An agent reports possible compromise on a pilot endpoint but the underlying evidence has not been inspected. What is the next step?","choices":["Corroborate the finding with available Defender/Intune telemetry and follow authorized triage before acting.","Immediately wipe the endpoint","Grant the agent unrestricted admin rights"],"why":["A supported Security Copilot agent may surface a threat-related finding. Treat generated interpretation as a lead; verify underlying Defender/Intune evidence, affected population, severity, timestamps and authorization before taking action. A recommendation is not proof of threat or change execution.","A generated finding alone is not sufficient justification for destructive action.","Broader permissions do not establish the finding's accuracy."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/copilot/"},{"id":"OPE-075","terms":[["Performance cohort","Defined population used for comparing endpoint metrics."],["Baseline","Reference measurement against which later observations are compared."],["Data freshness","Age of the observation relative to the relevant event."]],"mechanism":"Performance recommendations should be evaluated against actual data collection, cohort definition, sample size, application/OS versions and recent changes. Supported AI analysis can help identify patterns but cannot establish causation from correlation alone.","prereq":"Confirm eligible analytics/agent features, underlying data scope and time period. Compare startup/app reliability indicators before proposing a controlled test.","scenario":"An agent reports that startup has slowed, based on two devices after a recent update. What should IT do?","choices":["Check sample size, broader comparable cohort, timing and other changes before a bounded pilot intervention.","Roll back every update tenantwide immediately","Report confirmed causation from the agent text alone"],"why":["Performance recommendations should be evaluated against actual data collection, cohort definition, sample size, application/OS versions and recent changes. Supported AI analysis can help identify patterns but cannot establish causation from correlation alone.","Broad rollback exceeds the evidence.","Correlation and generated interpretation are not proof of causal mechanism."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/endpoint-analytics/"},{"id":"OPE-076","terms":[["Recommendation","Proposed action rather than an observed result."],["Change approval","Documented authorization for an applicable production change."],["Rollback","Planned supported recovery action if a change fails."]],"mechanism":"Agent recommendations should be validated against source evidence and policy scope. Decide whether the proposed setting is supported, why it addresses the problem, what else it affects, who approves and how the outcome is verified. Some agent capabilities or licensing change over time.","prereq":"Confirm current agent availability and permission boundary; use approved pilot, negative control, evidence threshold and rollback plan. Do not enable paid feature or write actions without authorization.","scenario":"An agent recommends disabling a broad security control to improve app compatibility. What is appropriate?","choices":["Investigate the specific compatibility problem and evaluate a narrow approved alternative with security review and evidence.","Accept the recommendation automatically","Treat recommendation text as proof devices already changed"],"why":["Agent recommendations should be validated against source evidence and policy scope. Decide whether the proposed setting is supported, why it addresses the problem, what else it affects, who approves and how the outcome is verified. Some agent capabilities or licensing change over time.","Blind approval can weaken security and bypass review.","Proposed action is not endpoint state."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/copilot/"},{"id":"OPE-077","terms":[["Discovery script","Supported script collecting values to evaluate in custom compliance."],["JSON rule","Structured rule specifying applicable discovered setting, expected type/operator and value."],["Custom compliance","Evaluation of a supported additional condition, not a repair operation."]],"mechanism":"Custom compliance discovers measured values and compares them with corresponding JSON rules. Output schema, data type, property name and execution context must match. A discovery failure or incompatible schema is not the same as a valid noncompliance result. Remediation is separate.","prereq":"Confirm supported platform and custom compliance requirements, approved script, matching rules and pilot target. Use benign test value and inspect discovered output/status.","scenario":"The discovery script emits a string for a rule expecting an integer. What is the best correction?","choices":["Align the discovered value type and rule schema, test output and retest the pilot.","Assign a different device name","Treat discovery script as automatic remediation"],"why":["Custom compliance discovers measured values and compares them with corresponding JSON rules. Output schema, data type, property name and execution context must match. A discovery failure or incompatible schema is not the same as a valid noncompliance result. Remediation is separate.","Display names do not fix schema mismatch.","Custom compliance evaluates conditions rather than automatically repairing them."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/device-security/compliance/custom-settings"},{"id":"OPE-078","terms":[["Denominator","Population represented by a metric or percentage."],["Report filter","Condition limiting records returned or displayed."],["Export","Transfer of supported report data for approved analysis."]],"mechanism":"A report represents a specific data source, scope and time. Compare assigned population, eligible endpoints, devices reporting and actual status; changing filters can change denominators. Use exports/workbooks/dashboard views for reproducible analysis without treating missing records as success.","prereq":"Define question, data source, scope, time, fields and access. Compare raw counts and freshness; protect sensitive data in exports.","scenario":"A report shows 90% compliance but excludes 40 devices with no recent check-in. What must the analyst disclose?","choices":["The measured denominator, excluded/stale records, time range and limits before claiming fleet-wide compliance.","Present 90% as all devices compliant","Delete nonreporting records to improve the metric"],"why":["A report represents a specific data source, scope and time. Compare assigned population, eligible endpoints, devices reporting and actual status; changing filters can change denominators. Use exports/workbooks/dashboard views for reproducible analysis without treating missing records as success.","The measured subset is not the entire fleet.","Deleting records distorts evidence and may remove managed assets."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/device-security/compliance/monitor-policy"},{"id":"OPE-079","terms":[["Endpoint Analytics","Supported reporting of endpoint performance/experience metrics."],["Startup performance","Applicable measurement of startup and sign-in experience."],["Collection policy","Applicable settings enabling required data collection."]],"mechanism":"Analytics metrics depend on eligible devices, collection settings, upload and processing. Startup or app reliability findings describe observed performance, not automatic proof that one Intune policy caused it. Compare cohorts and timing, then test a hypothesis.","prereq":"Verify supported device/entitlement, telemetry collection and service reachability. Compare baseline, sample and current metrics; use benign lab data.","scenario":"A newly enrolled device has no analytics score immediately. What should be checked?","choices":["Verify eligible collection policy, connectivity and processing time before diagnosing performance.","Wipe the device for missing score","Assume missing score proves excellent performance"],"why":["Analytics metrics depend on eligible devices, collection settings, upload and processing. Startup or app reliability findings describe observed performance, not automatic proof that one Intune policy caused it. Compare cohorts and timing, then test a hypothesis.","Wipe is disproportionate and does not establish collection eligibility.","Missing evidence is not positive performance evidence."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/endpoint-analytics/"},{"id":"OPE-080","terms":[["Detection script","Code evaluating a narrow condition and reporting the supported status."],["Remediation script","Code attempting to correct the detected issue."],["Idempotence","Safe repeated operation converging on the intended state without unnecessary additional change."]],"mechanism":"Supported Remediations use a detection result to determine whether repair is invoked. In the documented detection convention, exit 1 identifies an issue. Verify final state, re-detect, and ensure repeated runs are safe; execution context and schedule matter.","prereq":"Check entitlement, approved script/context, pilot targeting, reversible benign marker condition and schedule. Never run unreviewed destructive scripts; capture redacted output.","scenario":"A detection script identifies a missing benign marker but exits 0. Repair does not start. What should be fixed?","choices":["Use the documented exit 1 for the detected issue, then verify repair and re-detection.","Force every remediation to delete user data","Change a device scope tag to fix exit status"],"why":["Supported Remediations use a detection result to determine whether repair is invoked. In the documented detection convention, exit 1 identifies an issue. Verify final state, re-detect, and ensure repeated runs are safe; execution context and schedule matter.","Destructive repair is not justified by the narrow condition.","Scope tags do not control script return semantics."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/device-management/tools/deploy-remediations"},{"id":"OPE-081","terms":[["Reliability metric","Measure of observed stability such as supported app failures or restart patterns."],["User experience score","Derived indicator based on applicable measured data."],["Confounder","Another factor that could explain the observed metric change."]],"mechanism":"Reliability and experience measurements depend on population, versions, telemetry and time. A low score can reflect multiple causes such as app crashes, hardware, updates or usage. Correlate event evidence with changes and compare similar devices.","prereq":"Check current supported reporting and collection, cohort size, app/build and restart/failure evidence. Define a testable hypothesis and before/after comparison.","scenario":"A fleet reliability score falls after a policy deployment, but a major app update occurred simultaneously. What should IT do?","choices":["Compare affected cohorts, exact event timestamps and both changes before attributing cause.","Declare the policy caused every crash","Disable analytics to restore score"],"why":["Reliability and experience measurements depend on population, versions, telemetry and time. A low score can reflect multiple causes such as app crashes, hardware, updates or usage. Correlate event evidence with changes and compare similar devices.","Simultaneous changes are confounders.","Disabling measurement hides evidence, not the underlying issue."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/endpoint-analytics/"},{"id":"OPE-082","terms":[["Service health","Provider status and incident/advisory information for supported cloud services."],["Message center","Microsoft service communications about relevant changes."],["Operational baseline","Documented expected state and routine measurement for the environment."]],"mechanism":"Cloud-service incidents can produce correlated symptoms across otherwise healthy endpoints. Review service health, affected workload and time, compare local diagnostics and escalate using supported channels. Message center notices inform upcoming change preparation, not proof of current outage.","prereq":"Use authorized access to tenant communications; compare incident scope and local timestamps. Maintain a baseline and avoid destructive actions during unconfirmed outages.","scenario":"Several sites report identical management delays and normal network tests. What should be checked first?","choices":["Check service health/advisories and correlate affected workload and timing with local evidence.","Bulk re-enroll every device","Delete all configuration profiles"],"why":["Cloud-service incidents can produce correlated symptoms across otherwise healthy endpoints. Review service health, affected workload and time, compare local diagnostics and escalate using supported channels. Message center notices inform upcoming change preparation, not proof of current outage.","Bulk re-enrollment may disrupt unaffected devices without addressing provider incident.","Deleting policies destroys intended state without confirming root cause."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-365/enterprise/view-service-health"},{"id":"OPE-083","terms":[["Alert rule","Supported rule identifying a condition and notifying configured recipients."],["Compliance drift","Change away from expected compliant posture."],["Deduplication","Reducing repeated notifications for equivalent events where supported."]],"mechanism":"Operational alerts should identify actionable conditions, scope and responder. Set supported thresholds and evaluate actual telemetry freshness; avoid alert fatigue. Notifications are signals to investigate rather than automatic proof of root cause or policy success. Current alert feature support varies by workload.","prereq":"Check documented available alert/notification mechanisms, license, permissions, destinations and supported conditions. Define severity, owner and response runbook; test harmless signal.","scenario":"An alert fires repeatedly for the same stale device and no responder is assigned. How should the workflow be improved?","choices":["Define actionable supported thresholds, ownership, deduplication where available and evidence-based response.","Create more identical alerts","Automatically wipe every device generating an alert"],"why":["Operational alerts should identify actionable conditions, scope and responder. Set supported thresholds and evaluate actual telemetry freshness; avoid alert fatigue. Notifications are signals to investigate rather than automatic proof of root cause or policy success. Current alert feature support varies by workload.","Duplicate alerts increase noise without improving response.","Alert occurrence does not justify destructive action."],"source":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/device-security/compliance/monitor-policy"}]);
